WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.120 --> 00:00:03.200
I’m often asked
what my opinion

00:00:03.200 --> 00:00:05.760
about a diet
or disease is.

00:00:06.720 --> 00:00:12.080
Who cares what my or
anyone else’s opinion is.

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:16.160
All we should care about
is what the science says.

00:00:16.160 --> 00:00:18.240
What does the best available
balance of evidence

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:20.240
Published in the peer-
reviewed medical literature

00:00:20.240 --> 00:00:21.680
have to say right now?

00:00:22.560 --> 00:00:24.960
Welcome to the
Nutrition Facts Podcast,

00:00:24.960 --> 00:00:27.520
I’m your host,
Dr. Michael Greger.

00:00:28.320 --> 00:00:31.040
There has been a
history of enthusiasm

00:00:31.040 --> 00:00:33.600
for protein in the
nutrition world.

00:00:33.600 --> 00:00:36.640
In fact, a century ago the
protein recommendations

00:00:36.640 --> 00:00:39.257
were more than twice
what they are today.

00:00:39.760 --> 00:00:41.920
Let’s start with a
public health case

00:00:41.920 --> 00:00:45.760
for modernizing the definition
of protein quality.

00:00:47.200 --> 00:00:50.160
In 2019, Dr. David Katz
and colleagues—

00:00:50.160 --> 00:00:52.720
including one of my favorite
researchers, David Jenkins—

00:00:52.720 --> 00:00:55.440
published a public health
case for modernizing

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:58.240
the definition of protein quality.

00:00:58.240 --> 00:01:01.760
The prevailing definition seems to
have more to do with biochemistry

00:01:01.760 --> 00:01:04.160
than the net effects on human health.

00:01:04.160 --> 00:01:07.360
The popular concept that protein
is good, and the more the better,

00:01:07.360 --> 00:01:10.560
coupled with a protein quality
definition that favors animal protein,

00:01:11.120 --> 00:01:14.000
fosters the impression that eating
more meat, eggs, and dairy

00:01:14.000 --> 00:01:17.680
is desirable and preferable,
but this is directly opposed

00:01:17.680 --> 00:01:20.560
to nutrition guidelines that are
instead trying to push more plants.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:24.640
Although protein malnutrition is still
prevalent in many areas of the world,

00:01:24.640 --> 00:01:28.240
it is exceedingly rare
in the industrialized world,

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:30.240
where the most formidable
public health threat

00:01:30.240 --> 00:01:33.440
is not something like kwashiorkor—
protein/calorie malnutrition—

00:01:33.440 --> 00:01:35.520
but from chronic diseases.

00:01:36.080 --> 00:01:39.120
In 2016, a landmark study
was published out of Harvard,

00:01:39.120 --> 00:01:43.840
involving more than 100,000 people,
that found that replacing animal protein

00:01:43.840 --> 00:01:48.640
with plant protein was associated
with lower risk of dying prematurely.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.920
The worst seemed to be
processed meat like bacon,

00:01:51.920 --> 00:01:54.240
as well as egg protein,
the egg whites,

00:01:54.240 --> 00:01:58.800
but swapping in even just 3% plant
protein for any of the animal proteins—

00:01:58.800 --> 00:02:02.000
processed meat, unprocessed meat,
chicken, fish, eggs, or dairy—

00:02:02.000 --> 00:02:04.240
was associated with a
significantly lower risk

00:02:04.240 --> 00:02:07.840
of arguably the most important
endpoint of all: death.

00:02:08.480 --> 00:02:10.880
Yeah, but how do we
know it's the protein?

00:02:10.880 --> 00:02:14.080
The researchers adjusted for factors
such as saturated fat intake,

00:02:14.080 --> 00:02:16.560
which suggested it wasn't
just the animal fat.

00:02:17.360 --> 00:02:19.440
Okay, but how does your body
even know the difference

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:22.160
between protein from a plant
and protein from an animal?

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:23.520
Isn't protein, protein?

00:02:23.520 --> 00:02:27.200
No. Unlike animal protein,
plant protein is generally low

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:29.440
in branched-chain
amino acids, for example,

00:02:29.440 --> 00:02:32.000
and decreased consumption
of branched-chain amino acids

00:02:32.000 --> 00:02:33.760
improves metabolic health.

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:36.320
It could be the IGF-1,

00:02:36.320 --> 00:02:38.720
a cancer-promoting growth
hormone that is boosted

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:41.520
by so-called high-quality
animal protein intake, though.

00:02:42.080 --> 00:02:44.880
We suspect the IGF-1
connection is cause and effect,

00:02:44.880 --> 00:02:48.160
since people who are born
to have higher IGF-1 levels

00:02:48.160 --> 00:02:50.320
regardless of what they eat
do appear to suffer

00:02:50.320 --> 00:02:53.920
higher rates of killers like
type 2 diabetes and heart disease.

00:02:54.720 --> 00:02:57.680
Or it could be something the
Harvard researchers didn't control for

00:02:57.680 --> 00:03:00.560
such as toxic pollutants like
as dioxins and PBCs,

00:03:00.560 --> 00:03:03.680
since they tend to accumulate
up the food chain in cattle,

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:07.600
pigs, chickens, and fish,
and therefore end up on our plates.

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:10.240
So plant-based protein also
stands as an important step

00:03:10.240 --> 00:03:13.040
to lower the body burden
of harmful pollutants.

00:03:13.920 --> 00:03:16.400
If you don't think 100,000
people are enough,

00:03:16.400 --> 00:03:18.160
how about 400,000 people?

00:03:18.160 --> 00:03:23.760
The NIH-AARP study is the largest
diet cohort study in history.

00:03:23.760 --> 00:03:27.120
And again, simply swapping
3% of calories from various

00:03:27.120 --> 00:03:29.600
animal protein sources
with plant protein

00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:33.040
was associated with 10%
decreased overall mortality,

00:03:33.040 --> 00:03:36.480
and you get even twice that benefit
if you get rid of eggs, too.

00:03:37.920 --> 00:03:41.520
That's not a surprise, since
egg consumption is associated

00:03:41.520 --> 00:03:46.320
with a higher risk of developing
cardiovascular disease.

00:03:46.320 --> 00:03:50.240
Put all the studies together on
dietary protein intake and mortality,

00:03:50.240 --> 00:03:53.920
and people who eat more protein
tend to live shorter lives,

00:03:53.920 --> 00:03:58.000
but this is mainly driven by the
harmful association of animal protein.

00:03:58.000 --> 00:04:01.600
Plant protein intake is inversely
associated with mortality,

00:04:01.600 --> 00:04:05.040
meaning those who eat more plant
protein tend to live longer lives.

00:04:05.680 --> 00:04:08.400
More animal protein
may mean more mortality,

00:04:08.400 --> 00:04:11.600
whereas more plant protein
is correlated with less mortality.

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:14.080
So the best of both worlds
would be to increase

00:04:14.080 --> 00:04:17.440
the intake of plant protein
in place of animal protein.

00:04:17.440 --> 00:04:21.200
In other words, as another
2020 meta-analysis concluded,

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.720
"Persons should be encouraged to
increase their plant protein intake

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:27.840
to potentially decrease
their risk of death."

00:04:28.919 --> 00:04:32.632
Do vegetarians and vegans
get enough protein?

00:04:32.632 --> 00:04:35.308
Let's find out.

00:04:35.600 --> 00:04:39.600
The largest study in history
of those eating plant-based diets

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:44.160
recently compared the nutrient profiles
of about 30,000 non-vegetarians

00:04:44.160 --> 00:04:48.560
to 20,000 vegetarians and
about 5,000 vegans, flexitarians,

00:04:48.560 --> 00:04:51.200
and no meat except fish-eaters,

00:04:51.200 --> 00:04:54.240
allowing us to finally put
to rest the perennial question,

00:04:54.240 --> 00:04:56.640
“Do vegetarians get enough protein?”

00:04:57.360 --> 00:05:02.560
The average requirement is
42 grams of protein a day.

00:05:02.560 --> 00:05:08.880
Non-vegetarians get way more than
they need, and so does everyone else.

00:05:09.520 --> 00:05:14.800
On average, vegetarians and vegans get
70% more protein than they need every day.

00:05:16.080 --> 00:05:19.360
Surprising that there’s so much fuss
about protein in this country

00:05:19.360 --> 00:05:22.880
when less than 3% of adults
don’t make the cut,

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:28.960
presumably folks on extreme calorie-restricted
diets who just aren’t eating enough food, period.

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:33.600
But 97% of Americans get enough protein.

00:05:33.600 --> 00:05:40.080
There is a nutrient, though, for
which 97% of Americans are deficient.

00:05:40.080 --> 00:05:42.080
Now that’s a problem nutrient.

00:05:42.080 --> 00:05:43.920
That’s something we
really have to work on.

00:05:44.560 --> 00:05:52.080
Less than 3% of Americans get even the
recommended minimum adequate intake of…

00:05:52.080 --> 00:05:52.580
FIBER

00:05:53.440 --> 00:05:56.240
So the question isn’t
“where do you get your protein?”

00:05:56.240 --> 00:05:58.000
but “where do you get your fiber?”

00:05:58.640 --> 00:06:01.440
We only get about 15 grams a day.

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:04.800
The minimum daily requirement is 31.5

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:06.800
so we get less than
half the minimum.

00:06:07.360 --> 00:06:09.520
If you break it down
by age and gender,

00:06:09.520 --> 00:06:13.280
after studying the diets
of 12,761 Americans,

00:06:13.280 --> 00:06:16.800
the percent of men
between ages 14 and 50

00:06:16.800 --> 00:06:19.840
getting the minimum
adequate intake? Zero.

00:06:20.880 --> 00:06:26.400
This deficit is stunning in that dietary
fiber has been protectively associated

00:06:26.400 --> 00:06:28.880
with the risk of diabetes,
metabolic syndrome,

00:06:28.880 --> 00:06:31.920
cardiovascular disease, obesity,
and various cancers,

00:06:31.920 --> 00:06:35.200
as well high cholesterol, blood
pressure, and blood sugars.

00:06:35.200 --> 00:06:38.400
Therefore, it is not surprising
that fiber is now listed

00:06:38.400 --> 00:06:43.520
as a nutrient of concern in the dietary
guidelines. Protein is not.

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:48.160
One problem is that most people have
no idea what’s in their food.

00:06:48.160 --> 00:06:53.040
More than half of Americans think
steak is a significant fiber source.

00:06:54.160 --> 00:06:59.440
By definition, fiber is only found in plants.

00:06:59.440 --> 00:07:05.280
There is no fiber in meat, dairy or eggs,
and little or no fiber in junk food.

00:07:06.080 --> 00:07:09.040
Therein lies the problem.

00:07:09.920 --> 00:07:13.440
Americans should be eating more beans,
vegetables, fruits, whole grains.

00:07:13.440 --> 00:07:14.560
How are we doing on that?

00:07:14.560 --> 00:07:21.760
Well, 96% of Americans don’t eat the
recommended minimum daily intake of beans,

00:07:22.560 --> 00:07:26.160
96% don’t eat the measly minimum for greens,

00:07:26.160 --> 00:07:28.400
99% don’t get enough whole grains.

00:07:28.400 --> 00:07:29.760
Look at these numbers.

00:07:29.760 --> 00:07:35.360
Nearly the entire U.S. population fails
to eat enough whole plant foods.

00:07:36.160 --> 00:07:43.840
And it’s not getting better, a lack
of progress that’s disappointing.

00:07:44.800 --> 00:07:49.360
Even semi-vegetarians, though,
make the minimum for fiber.

00:07:49.360 --> 00:07:53.760
And those eating completely plant-based
diets triple the average American intake.

00:07:54.800 --> 00:07:59.120
Now when closing the fiber gap,
you’ll want to do it gradually,

00:07:59.120 --> 00:08:02.720
no more than about five extra
grams of fiber a day each week

00:08:02.720 --> 00:08:04.800
until you can kind of work your way up.

00:08:05.440 --> 00:08:07.040
But it’s worth it.

00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:10.240
Plant-derived diets tend
to contribute significantly

00:08:10.240 --> 00:08:13.360
less fat, saturated fat, cholesterol,
and foodborne pathogens,

00:08:13.360 --> 00:08:17.040
while at the same time offering more fiber,
folate, vitamin C, and phytochemicals,

00:08:17.040 --> 00:08:21.840
all essential factors for disease prevention,
and optimal health and well being.

00:08:22.560 --> 00:08:26.960
And the more plant foods, the better–
whole plant foods, the better.

00:08:26.960 --> 00:08:31.920
If you compare the nutritional quality
of vegan versus vegetarian,

00:08:31.920 --> 00:08:35.280
semi-vegetarian, pesco-vegetarian
and omnivorous diets,

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:39.200
traditional healthy diet indexing systems,

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:40.960
like compliance with
the dietary guidelines,

00:08:40.960 --> 00:08:47.280
consistently indicate the most plant-based
diet as the most healthy one.

00:08:49.040 --> 00:08:51.200
Finally today, we
examine the myth

00:08:51.200 --> 00:08:54.576
that plant proteins
are incomplete.

00:08:55.200 --> 00:08:58.800
All nutrients come from
the sun or the soil.

00:08:58.800 --> 00:09:03.200
Vitamin D, the "sunshine vitamin," is created
when skin is exposed to sunlight.

00:09:03.200 --> 00:09:05.920
Everything else comes
from the ground.

00:09:05.920 --> 00:09:07.280
Minerals originate from the earth,

00:09:07.280 --> 00:09:11.120
and vitamins from the plants and
micro-organisms that grow from it.

00:09:11.120 --> 00:09:15.360
The calcium in a cow's milk
(and her 200-pound skeleton)

00:09:15.920 --> 00:09:19.600
came from all the plants she ate,
which drew it up from the soil.

00:09:20.160 --> 00:09:24.880
We can cut out the middle-moo, though,
and get calcium from the plants directly.

00:09:25.840 --> 00:09:27.200
Where do you get
your protein?

00:09:28.240 --> 00:09:30.640
Protein contains essential amino acids,

00:09:30.640 --> 00:09:34.720
meaning our bodies can't make them
and so are essential to get from our diet.

00:09:34.720 --> 00:09:37.760
But other animals
don't make them either.

00:09:37.760 --> 00:09:42.480
All essential amino acids originate
from plants (and microbes),

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:45.600
and all plant proteins have
all essential amino acids.

00:09:45.600 --> 00:09:50.400
The only truly "incomplete" protein
in the food supply is gelatin,

00:09:50.400 --> 00:09:53.520
which is missing the amino
acid tryptophan.

00:09:53.520 --> 00:09:58.240
So the only protein source that
you couldn't live on is Jell-O.

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:02.560
As I covered previously, those
eating plant-based diets

00:10:03.120 --> 00:10:06.320
average about twice the average
daily requirement for protein.

00:10:07.040 --> 00:10:10.480
Those who don't know where to get
protein on a plant-based diet

00:10:10.480 --> 00:10:13.120
don't know beans!
Get it?

00:10:13.920 --> 00:10:18.240
That's protein quantity, though.
What about protein quality?

00:10:18.240 --> 00:10:21.680
The concept that plant protein was
inferior to animal protein

00:10:21.680 --> 00:10:26.560
arose from studies performed on
rodents more than a century ago.

00:10:27.280 --> 00:10:32.000
Scientists found that infant rats
don't grow as well on plants.

00:10:32.000 --> 00:10:36.480
But infant rats don't grow as well
on human breast milk either,

00:10:37.120 --> 00:10:39.920
so does that mean we shouldn't
breastfeed our babies?

00:10:39.920 --> 00:10:41.600
Ridiculous! They're rats.

00:10:42.240 --> 00:10:47.680
Rat milk has ten times more
protein than human milk,

00:10:47.680 --> 00:10:53.280
because rats grow about ten times
faster than human infants.

00:10:53.920 --> 00:10:56.400
It's true that some plant
proteins are relatively low

00:10:56.400 --> 00:10:58.480
in certain essential amino acids.

00:10:58.480 --> 00:11:04.320
So about 40 years ago, the myth of
"protein combining" came into vogueâ€”

00:11:05.280 --> 00:11:09.280
literally: the February '75 issue
of Vogue magazine.

00:11:09.280 --> 00:11:13.440
The concept was that we needed to eat
"complementary proteins" together,

00:11:13.440 --> 00:11:17.360
for example rice and beans,
to make up for their relative shortfalls.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:24.160
This fallacy was refuted decades ago,
the myth that plant proteins are incomplete,

00:11:24.160 --> 00:11:28.240
that plant proteins aren't as good,
that one has to combine proteins at meals.

00:11:28.240 --> 00:11:32.640
These have all been dismissed by
the nutrition community as myths

00:11:32.640 --> 00:11:37.360
decades ago, but many in medicine
evidently didn't get the memo.

00:11:38.240 --> 00:11:41.440
Dr. John McDougall called out
the American Heart Association

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:45.440
for a 2001 publication that questioned
the completeness of plant proteins.

00:11:46.000 --> 00:11:49.680
Thankfully though they've changed
and acknowledged now

00:11:49.680 --> 00:11:52.880
that plant proteins can provide
all the essential amino acids;

00:11:52.880 --> 00:11:56.240
no need to combine
complementary proteins.

00:11:56.240 --> 00:11:59.120
It turns out our body
is not stupid.

00:11:59.120 --> 00:12:02.480
It maintains pools of free
amino acids that can be used

00:12:02.480 --> 00:12:04.880
to do all the complementing for us,

00:12:05.440 --> 00:12:09.920
not to mention the massive protein
recycling program our body has.

00:12:09.920 --> 00:12:14.000
Some 90 grams of protein is dumped
into the digestive tract every day

00:12:14.000 --> 00:12:18.080
from our own body to get broken
back down and reassembled,

00:12:18.080 --> 00:12:22.560
so our body can mix and match amino
acids to whatever proportions we need,

00:12:22.560 --> 00:12:27.520
whatever we eat, making it practically
impossible to even design a diet

00:12:27.520 --> 00:12:32.400
of whole plant foods that's sufficient
in calories, but deficient in protein.

00:12:32.400 --> 00:12:39.360
Thus, plant-based consumers do not need
to be AT ALL concerned about amino acid

00:12:39.360 --> 00:12:43.137
imbalances from the plant proteins
that make up our usual diets.

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:47.680
